The stopping voltage increases by approximately 1.035 V when the wavelength decreases from 400 nm to 300 nm.

The photoelectric effect equation gives the stopping voltage \(V_{\text{stop}} = \frac{hc}{e} \left( \frac{1}{\lambda} \right) - \frac{\phi}{e}\). The change in stopping voltage depends only on the change in wavelength: \(\Delta V = \frac{hc}{e} \left( \frac{1}{\lambda_2} - \frac{1}{\lambda_1} \right)\). Using \(h = 6.63 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J·s}\), \(c = 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\), and \(e = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\), we find \(\frac{hc}{e} = 1243.125 \, \text{V·nm}\). Substituting \(\lambda_1 = 400 \, \text{nm}\) and \(\lambda_2 = 300 \, \text{nm}\), we get \(\frac{1}{\lambda_2} - \frac{1}{\lambda_1} = \frac{1}{1200} \, \text{nm}^{-1}\), so \(\Delta V = 1243.125 / 1200 \approx 1.035 \, \text{V}\). Since the wavelength decreases, the stopping voltage increases.

