For an ideal gas at constant volume, the heat required to raise the temperature by ΔT is \( Q = n C_v \Delta T \), where \( C_v \) is the molar heat capacity at constant volume. Since the containers have the same volume, pressure, and temperature initially, the number of moles \( n \) is identical for both gases. Hydrogen (\( H_2 \)) is a diatomic rigid molecule with 5 degrees of freedom (3 translational, 2 rotational), so \( C_v = \frac{5}{2}R \). Ammonia (\( NH_3 \)) is a nonlinear polyatomic rigid molecule with 6 degrees of freedom (3 translational, 3 rotational), so \( C_v = 3R \). Given that 5 J of heat raises the temperature of hydrogen by ΔT, we have \( Q_H = n \cdot \frac{5}{2}R \Delta T = 5 \, \text{J} \), which gives \( nR\Delta T = 2 \, \text{J} \). For ammonia, the required heat is \( Q_A = n \cdot 3R \Delta T = 3 \times 2 = 6 \, \text{J} \). Thus, 6 J of heat must be transferred to ammonia to achieve the same temperature increase.

